  "worked_solution": "The polar curve $r=\\frac{13}{5}$ is a circle of radius $\\frac{13}{5}$ centered at the origin. It crosses the positive $y$-axis at the point $(0,\\frac{13}{5}),$ as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005246/assets/86158ced718c422c9b02d620ca4544eb7d2ff0e110a04fe4b7f749160fc46649.png)\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection in Cartesian coordinates is $(0,\\frac{13}{5}\\,).$",
